Done. Here's what I did:

Problem identified: check_standalone_destruction in src/safety.rs used a hardcoded list of 6 system path prefixes (/dev/, /etc/, /var/, /usr/, /boot/, /sys/) while the canonical CRITICAL_SYSTEM_DIRS constant contains 10 directories. This left 6 critical directories unprotected against truncate, shred, and wipefs:
• /bin/ — core system binaries
• /sbin/ — system admin binaries
• /lib/ — shared libraries
• /lib64/ — 64-bit shared libraries
• /opt/ — installed software
• /srv/ — server data

Fix: Replaced the hardcoded path checks with iteration over CRITICAL_SYSTEM_DIRS (matching both dir/... paths and bare directory names), keeping /dev/ and /sys/ as extras specific to this check.

Tests: Added 8 new assertions covering all previously-unprotected directories — both with subpaths (shred /bin/bash) and bare names (`shred /bin`).

